::GEO_U06_Pretest_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_Pretest_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which coordinate evidence can prove two sides of a quadrilateral are parallel? {
=A. The two sides have equal slopes#Equal slopes support that the two sides are parallel in a coordinate proof.
~B. The two sides have different midpoints#Different midpoints do not prove parallel sides.
~C. The figure looks balanced on the graph#Appearance is not enough for coordinate proof.
~D. The coordinates are all positive#Positive coordinates do not determine side relationships.
}

::GEO_U06_Pretest_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_Pretest_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.9<br><br>Question:<br>Which formula is most useful for finding the length of a segment between two coordinate points? {
~A. Slope formula#Slope gives direction or steepness, not the length of the segment.
=B. Distance formula#The distance formula finds the length between two points on the coordinate plane.
~C. Circle area formula#Circle area does not find the length of a coordinate segment.
~D. Volume formula#Volume formulas measure three-dimensional space, not coordinate segment length.
}

::GEO_U06_Pretest_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_Pretest_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.9<br><br>Question:<br>What is the equation of a circle with center (0, 0) and radius 7? {
~A. x + y = 7#This is a linear equation, not a circle equation.
~B. x&sup2; + y&sup2; = 7#The right side should be r&sup2;, not r.
=C. x&sup2; + y&sup2; = 49#For a circle centered at the origin, x&sup2; + y&sup2; = r&sup2;, and 7&sup2; = 49.
~D. (x - 7)&sup2; + y&sup2; = 49#This equation has center (7, 0), not (0, 0).
}

::GEO_U06_Pretest_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_Pretest_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.10<br><br>Question:<br>What is the center of the circle (x - 4)&sup2; + (y + 6)&sup2; = 25? {
~A. (-4, 6)#This reverses both signs from the equation form.
~B. (4, 6)#The x-coordinate is correct, but the y-coordinate sign is not.
~C. (-4, -6)#The y-coordinate is correct, but the x-coordinate sign is not.
=D. (4, -6)#The equation uses (x - h)&sup2; + (y - k)&sup2;, so the center is (4, -6).
}

::GEO_U06_Pretest_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_Pretest_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.10<br><br>Question:<br>A coordinate rectangle has side lengths 9 units and 5 units. What is its area? {
=A. 45 square units#Rectangle area is length times width, so 9(5) = 45 square units.
~B. 28 units#This is the perimeter, not the area.
~C. 14 square units#This adds the dimensions instead of multiplying them.
~D. 90 square units#This doubles the correct area without a geometric reason.
}

::GEO_U06_Pretest_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_Pretest_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.13<br><br>Question:<br>A triangular sign has base 10 ft and height 6 ft. What is its area? {
~A. 60 ft&sup2;#This omits the one-half factor in the triangle area formula.
=B. 30 ft&sup2;#Triangle area is <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>bh, so <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>(10)(6) = 30.
~C. 16 ft&sup2;#This adds base and height instead of finding area.
~D. 32 ft&sup2;#This does not match the triangle area formula.
}

::GEO_U06_Pretest_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_Pretest_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.15<br><br>Question:<br>Which unit is appropriate for the surface area of a rectangular prism? {
~A. centimeters#Centimeters are linear units used for length.
~B. cubic centimeters#Cubic centimeters measure volume, not surface area.
=C. square centimeters#Surface area measures the outside area of a solid, so square units are appropriate.
~D. degrees#Degrees measure angles, not surface area.
}

::GEO_U06_Pretest_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_Pretest_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.14<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression gives the volume of a cylinder with radius 3 and height 8? {
~A. 2&pi;(3)&sup2; + 2&pi;(3)(8)#This expression gives cylinder surface area, not volume.
~B. 2&pi;(3)(8)#This is lateral surface area, not volume.
~C. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>&pi;(3)&sup2;(8)#The one-third factor is for a cone, not a cylinder.
=D. &pi;(3)&sup2;(8)#Cylinder volume is V = &pi;r&sup2;h.
}

::GEO_U06_Pretest_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_Pretest_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A solid is enlarged by a scale factor of 3. How does its volume change? {
=A. It becomes 27 times as large#Volume changes by k&sup3;, so 3&sup3; = 27.
~B. It becomes 9 times as large#That is the area or surface area effect, not the volume effect.
~C. It becomes 3 times as large#That is the linear measurement effect.
~D. It becomes 6 times as large#Doubling the scale factor is not the volume scale rule.
}

::GEO_U06_Pretest_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_Pretest_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.11, MLA.GEO.CGM.12, MLA.GEO.CGM.16<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly matches a spatial modeling idea? {
~A. A cylinder sliced parallel to its circular bases forms a rectangle#A base-parallel slice of a cylinder forms a circle, not a rectangle.
=B. Rotating a rectangle around one side can form a cylinder#A rectangle rotating around one side sweeps out a cylinder, which correctly matches a solid of rotation.
~C. A conic section is formed by slicing a rectangular prism#Conic sections are formed by slicing cones, not rectangular prisms.
~D. A cross-section must always be three-dimensional#A cross-section is two-dimensional because it is the shape formed by a slice.
}
